WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
This is the protein structure lecture for KB8029, Bioinformatics, given by Petras Kundrotas.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:16.325
It covers what a protein structure is and how it is described, how two structures are compared, how structures are predicted, and how they are classified.

3
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:19.950
This lecture is about protein structure.

4
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:27.450
Chapter thirteen of the Pevsner book covers the same ground and is worth reading, because some of the quiz questions come from it.

5
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.325
But not all of them.

6
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:36.300
There is material in this lecture that the book does not cover, and some quiz and exam questions will come only from here.

7
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.350
Lectures on protein structure traditionally begin with the four levels of structure, and this one will too.

8
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:53.800
The emphasis, though, is on how proteins are built: how atoms become the bricks of life, with the primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary levels introduced along the way.

9
00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:01.800
Protein atoms can be arranged in a great many ways, so the second part of the lecture asks how to measure whether two structures are similar.

10
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.325
Tertiary and quaternary structures need different answers, so they are treated separately.

11
00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:11.875
The third part is about predicting those arrangements by computation.

12
00:01:11.875 --> 00:01:20.725
It is a sketch rather than a treatment — the subject deserves a course of its own — and it covers the methods that came before artificial intelligence.

13
00:01:20.725 --> 00:01:23.925
The modern approaches come later in this course.

14
00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:31.725
The lecture closes with the main ways of classifying tertiary structures, and with the databases that hold structural information.

15
00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.825
Start with what a protein is.

16
00:01:34.825 --> 00:01:38.450
Intuitively, a protein is a large molecule.

17
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:47.400
The two on screen make the point: alcohol dehydrogenase has two thousand six hundred and forty-three atoms, insulin four hundred and eleven.

18
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.850
They span roughly sixty-five and thirty-four ångström across.

19
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.475
Zoom in, though, and colour the atoms by element, and something simpler appears.

20
00:01:57.475 --> 00:02:00.275
There are only a few kinds of atom here.

21
00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:02.825
Five, in fact.

22
00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:09.900
For all their apparent complexity, proteins are built from carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, hydrogen and sulphur.

23
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.875
Each has a job.

24
00:02:11.875 --> 00:02:15.525
Carbon and nitrogen form the long polymer chains.

25
00:02:15.525 --> 00:02:24.650
Nitrogen, oxygen and hydrogen form hydrogen bonds and carry charge, which is one of the main things holding a three-dimensional structure together.

26
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.700
Sulphur forms disulphide bonds, which are stronger still.

27
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.100
Structures are often drawn without the hydrogens.

28
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.625
When that is done, the rest are called the heavy atoms — every atom except hydrogen.

29
00:02:38.225 --> 00:02:43.350
The first thing to say about a protein is that it is a flexible polymer chain.

30
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.500
Take the word polymer first.

31
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.900
The lower picture shows a fragment of the chain, running from a nitrogen to a carbon.

32
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.650
That fragment is called a residue, and it can repeat as many times as a protein needs.

33
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.250
The chain it builds is the backbone.

34
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.000
One caution about vocabulary.

35
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.600
Residue is often used as a synonym for amino acid, and that is not quite right.

36
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.100
Notice the bond leaving the alpha carbon with nothing on the end of it.

37
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:21.350
Something is attached there — a side chain — and the repeating backbone fragment together with its side chain is what makes an amino acid.

38
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.175
Now the word flexible.

39
00:03:24.175 --> 00:03:29.650
The chain can rotate about two angles, psi and phi, marked by the circular arrows.

40
00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:40.375
These are called dihedral angles, and the animation shows what one of them does: how the position of a backbone nitrogen changes relative to the three atoms before it in the chain.

41
00:03:40.375 --> 00:03:46.900
There is a third angle, omega, about the carbon–nitrogen bond, and that one barely rotates at all.

42
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.275
The reason lies in how the electrons are distributed in that bond.

43
00:03:51.275 --> 00:04:02.900
In practice omega takes one of two values: a hundred and eighty degrees, the trans form, almost always, and zero degrees, the cis form, in about three tenths of a per cent of cases.

44
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.725
Here are the same two proteins again, drawn as backbone only.

45
00:04:07.725 --> 00:04:10.750
A backbone alone is not a protein, though.

46
00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.475
It is a skeleton: necessary, but not sufficient.

47
00:04:15.075 --> 00:04:20.375
Add the side chains, drawn here as thin lines, and the skeleton gets its flesh.

48
00:04:20.375 --> 00:04:25.825
Now it is a protein — a large molecule that does something in a living organism.

49
00:04:26.425 --> 00:04:33.975
The amino acids fall into three groups, shown here with their full names and their three-letter and one-letter abbreviations.

50
00:04:33.975 --> 00:04:38.325
The hydrophobic side chains are made almost entirely of carbon.

51
00:04:38.325 --> 00:04:44.150
A carbon atom has a symmetric cloud of electrons, so it cannot form a hydrogen bond.

52
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:56.900
There are two apparent exceptions — the sulphur in methionine and the nitrogen in tryptophan — but neither can form hydrogen bonds either: the sulphur is surrounded by carbons, and the nitrogen sits inside a ring.

53
00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.200
The polar and charged side chains are different.

54
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.125
They contain nitrogen and oxygen, and those can form hydrogen bonds.

55
00:05:05.725 --> 00:05:11.300
The side chains divide first by whether they can make a hydrogen bond, and these cannot.

56
00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:18.525
Leucine, isoleucine and methionine are plain hydrocarbon chains; phenylalanine is a ring of the same kind.

57
00:05:18.525 --> 00:05:27.525
They have no partial charges worth speaking of, so water does not want them, and in a folded protein they are usually found pressed together in the core.

58
00:05:27.525 --> 00:05:32.350
That one fact explains a great deal about what shapes a protein can take.

59
00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:41.500
These can make a hydrogen bond, because each carries an oxygen, a nitrogen or a sulphur with a hydrogen attached or a lone pair to offer.

60
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:58.225
Tyrosine, asparagine, cysteine, histidine and serine are comfortable on the surface in contact with water, and they are also the residues that do most of the chemistry: a hydrogen bond donor or acceptor in the right place is what an active site is usually made of.

61
00:05:58.825 --> 00:06:03.525
A third group goes further and carries a charge at the pH of a cell.

62
00:06:04.125 --> 00:06:10.800
The acidic residues, aspartic acid and glutamic acid, lose a proton and become negative.

63
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.600
The basic ones, lysine and arginine, gain one and become positive.

64
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.775
Opposite charges attract across the surface of a protein and hold parts of it together, and like charges push apart.

65
00:06:22.775 --> 00:06:33.375
This is also where a pH change does its work: shift the pH far enough and histidine in particular changes its charge, which is how a number of proteins switch behaviour.

66
00:06:33.975 --> 00:06:39.925
Join amino acids together and you have a polypeptide chain, as long as it needs to be.

67
00:06:39.925 --> 00:06:44.250
The order of the amino acids along it is the primary structure.

68
00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.050
A chain on its own does nothing, though.

69
00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:55.925
It only becomes a protein in water, and it is water that performs the trick: it turns a floppy chain into the structures we look at in PyMOL.

70
00:06:56.525 --> 00:07:03.025
Switch PyMOL to the cartoon representation and parts of the structure are drawn differently from the rest.

71
00:07:03.025 --> 00:07:07.900
Those parts are the rigid elements: alpha helices and beta strands.

72
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.025
Look closely at the first of them.

73
00:07:11.025 --> 00:07:21.325
An alpha helix is drawn as a spiral in every viewer, and it is held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone nitrogen and backbone oxygen, four residues apart.

74
00:07:21.325 --> 00:07:23.550
Note the word backbone.

75
00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:29.375
The nitrogens and oxygens of the side chains take no part in holding a helix together.

76
00:07:29.975 --> 00:07:38.925
The other rigid element is a stretch of backbone in an extended conformation, where the dihedral angles are all close to a hundred and eighty degrees.

77
00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:43.800
That is a beta strand, and every viewer draws it as a thick flat arrow.

78
00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.325
Strands do not occur alone.

79
00:07:46.325 --> 00:07:59.950
They pair up into beta sheets, held together again by hydrogen bonds between backbone nitrogen and backbone oxygen — but this time between neighbouring strands, and it does not matter how many residues separate them along the chain.

80
00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.475
As with the helix, the side chains take no part.

